WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
Course graphics should explain something.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:06.350
Useful eLearning graphics include process flows, comparison charts, checklists,

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:10.930
visual summaries, concept maps, decision trees, and diagrams that show relationships.

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.530
A graphic that only fills space may make a screen look complete,

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.520
but it does not necessarily help learning.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.720
The best graphics earn their place by making a difficult idea easier to understand.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:22.535
A practical production choice is raster versus vector.

00:00:22.535 --> 00:00:26.710
Raster graphics are pixel-based and are commonly used for photos, screenshots, textures,

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:27.800
and detailed images.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.765
Vector graphics are shape-based and can often scale cleanly,

00:00:30.765 --> 00:00:35.020
which makes them useful for icons, diagrams, line art, and simple illustrations.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.250
The format should match the use case and the expected screen size.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.645
Export formats matter.

00:00:39.645 --> 00:00:42.950
PNG is often useful for crisp screen graphics with transparency.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.710
JPG may be appropriate for photographic images where file size matters.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:51.120
SVG can be useful for scalable vector artwork when the delivery environment supports it.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.425
PDF can work well for downloadable references.

00:00:53.425 --> 00:00:55.640
These are practical choices, not universal rules,

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.030
and they should be tested in the course environment.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.422
Consistent templates and reusable components improve quality and speed.

00:01:01.422 --> 00:01:04.675
A course may use a standard diagram style, repeated icon treatment,

00:01:04.675 --> 00:01:07.240
consistent callout boxes, and predictable spacing.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.305
Reuse helps learners recognize patterns, and it helps designers update assets later.

00:01:11.305 --> 00:01:13.815
Consistency should not make every screen identical,

00:01:13.815 --> 00:01:15.680
but it should make the course feel coherent.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.342
Design graphics for the destination.

00:01:17.342 --> 00:01:20.170
A 16:9 slide, a responsive lesson block,

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.955
and a downloadable handout may require different compositions.

00:01:22.955 --> 00:01:25.270
Text inside a graphic must stay readable.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.120
Diagrams should survive resizing.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.815
A visual summary should remain useful outside the course if learners download it as a

00:01:30.815 --> 00:01:31.217
reference.

00:01:31.217 --> 00:01:34.240
Production begins with understanding where the asset will live.
